course: dictionary
lesson: 9612
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Read the words around the word first, because they are the clues. The word stroke is a good example.
explain: «Stroke the cat gently, in the direction its fur grows.» Here stroke means to move your hand gently over something, again and again. Try that meaning in the sentence. It fits, so you know you are right.
explain: «She swam the front crawl with long, smooth strokes.» Here a stroke is one movement of the arms. The word swam is the clue: it is about water, not about a cat.
explain: «The bell rang on the stroke of twelve.» Here a stroke is one ring of the bell. A stroke can also be a hit in tennis, or a stroke of luck: a sudden piece of good fortune. Always check which meaning fits the sentence.
fact: Stroke can be a verb (to stroke, strokes, stroked, stroking) or a noun (a stroke, strokes).
fact: «Stroke» means to move your hand gently over an animal or person, usually again and again.
fact: «Stroke» also means one movement of a pen or brush, or of an arm or oar in swimming or rowing.
fact: «Stroke» means a hit in tennis, golf or cricket, and «a stroke of luck» means a sudden piece of good luck.
fact: A stroke is also a serious illness when blood cannot get to part of the brain. If you think someone is having one, call emergency services at once.
exercise: «She swam the front crawl with long, smooth strokes.» What is a stroke here? | A ring of a clock | A gentle touch on the arm | *One movement of the arms in swimming | A hit with a bat
exercise: «The bell rang on the stroke of twelve.» What does stroke mean here? | A gentle touch | A hit in cricket | A movement of a paintbrush | *One ring of the bell
exercise: «Her pen made a clean stroke across the page.» What does stroke mean here? | A hit in golf | A serious illness | A gentle touch on a cat | *One movement of a pen
test: «Always ask the owner before you stroke a dog.» What does stroke mean here? | To throw a ball for it | To hit it with a stick | *To move your hand gently over it | To run after it
test: Jade says, «It was a stroke of luck that we found the lost ticket.» What does stroke of luck mean? | A serious illness | A movement of a brush | *A sudden piece of good luck | A hit in tennis
exam: Mum says, «His face drooped and his speech was slurred, so he may be having a stroke.» What is a stroke here? | *A serious illness when blood cannot reach part of the brain | A gentle touch on the arm | One stroke of a swimmer's arm | A hit in tennis
